WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:02.880
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:13.060
NARRATOR: 530 million
miles from the Sun,

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:16.450
a tiny lump of ice
and rock starts

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:18.970
its journey towards
the inner solar system.

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:25.390
 Scientifically, comets are
more precious than pure gold.

00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:27.430
They are actual time capsules.

00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:30.100
They are preservations of what
the chemistry, the environment

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:32.830
was like when our
solar system formed.

00:00:32.830 --> 00:00:35.650
And they hold clues as
to how our chemistry

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:36.940
arrived here on Earth.

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:39.140
Comets are some of the most
valuable scientific treasures

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:39.940
that exist.

00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:45.940
NARRATOR: Finding a comet we
can study is not an easy task.

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:47.500
 Finding the right
comet for a mission

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:50.140
is kind of like you're
auditioning them, right?

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:53.050
One comes in, and it's
like, oh, yeah, or it's OK,

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:55.350
but it's not exactly
what we're looking for.

00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:57.620
And oh, this one's too
big, this one's too little.

00:00:57.620 --> 00:00:58.570
This one's too active.

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:02.980
 What we really want
to see is that comet

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:05.470
come from outer parts
of the solar system

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:07.750
and get heated up as it
gets close to the sun,

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:10.360
pass around the sun, and head
back out to the outer part

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:11.330
of the solar system.

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:13.480
So you need to get way
out in the solar system

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:15.280
and catch comet on its way in.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:19.050
NARRATOR: Comet
67P fits the bill.

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:22.960
 The special thing
about comet 67P

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:25.270
is how accessible
it is here on Earth.

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:28.450
It actually orbits around the
sun once every 6 and 1/2 years,

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:30.670
and its orbit doesn't
take it all that far out.

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:32.770
Only about as far out
as the planet Jupiter.

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:35.320
That gives us many chances
to actually reach the comet

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:36.760
and successfully
rendezvous with it.

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:41.470
ASTRONAUT: [INAUDIBLE]

00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:44.290
NARRATOR: 67Ps moment in
the spotlight arrived.

00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:48.650
The European Space
Agency launched the

00:01:48.650 --> 00:01:50.540
groundbreaking Rosetta probe.

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:55.040
 For years, we've
studied comets from afar.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:57.980
We had never seen one up
close with cutting edge

00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:00.320
technology to learn
about how comets

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:02.220
behave as they orbit the sun.

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:10.850
NARRATOR: Missions to large
planet-sized objects are hard.

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:13.600
The Hubble Space
Telescope blurry images

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:16.180
revealed Rosetta's
target is just

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:21.160
two miles wide, and not just
a small target, a moving one.

00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:26.500
Comet 67P races through
the solar system

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:31.050
at over 33,000 miles an hour.

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:33.770
 The precision involved
is pretty incredible.

00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:36.210
It's like making that hole in
one golf shot from New York

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:37.680
to San Francisco.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:42.410
NARRATOR: But catching comet
67P wasn't just a straight shot.

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:46.740
 The spacecraft had to actually
get the same sort of velocity

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:49.650
as the comet, and with
current propulsion systems,

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:52.440
we can't achieve that by
flying directly to the comet.

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:59.310
NARRATOR: Instead, Rosetta
performed a slingshot maneuver

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:00.600
that would take 10 years.

00:03:04.393 --> 00:03:05.560
 So what it's
doing, it's actually

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:07.810
taking some energy
away from the planet

00:03:07.810 --> 00:03:10.210
so it's slowing the
planets down a little bit

00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:13.000
and then imparting that
energy into the probe.

00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:19.750
NARRATOR: Rosetta flew past
Jupiter nearly 100 million

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:23.410
miles from the warmth
of the sun and entered

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:29.200
the most dangerous part of
its journey, hibernation.

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:31.120
 Even though Rosetta
had solar panels on it,

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:33.790
it still had to be put to
sleep, and the reason for that

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:37.240
is that it had to track 67P
a ways away from the sun

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:38.860
and there just was
not enough collection

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:43.270
area and solar power to be
able to power the instruments.

00:03:43.270 --> 00:03:45.740
 There's no way to put
spacecraft into hibernation,

00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:47.830
but the worrying thing
at the back of your mind

00:03:47.830 --> 00:03:50.020
is that it has never been
done for this long before.

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:53.470
NARRATOR: 31 months
of hibernation

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:55.780
gave Rosetta's
team plenty of time

00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:58.870
to worry about what
could go wrong.

00:03:58.870 --> 00:04:01.240
 It's dark and cold
out there in space.

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:02.810
There's a lot of
things going on.

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:04.990
There's a lot of little
micrometeorites out there,

00:04:04.990 --> 00:04:07.990
and without the
constant communication

00:04:07.990 --> 00:04:11.830
with that spacecraft, it just
is a little nerve wracking when

00:04:11.830 --> 00:04:13.630
the day comes and it's
time to flip the switch

00:04:13.630 --> 00:04:15.490
and turn it back on.

00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:19.630
NARRATOR: January 20th, 2014.

00:04:19.630 --> 00:04:23.650
After almost 10 years in space,
it was finally time for Rosetta

00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:27.770
to wake up, reactivate
its communication system,

00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:28.570
and phone home.

00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:34.300
 I mean the tension
was just palpable.

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:36.280
I mean, how long do we
have to wait before we're

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:37.710
going to get the signal.

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:38.810
SIMON SHERIDAN:
It's got to work.

00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:39.850
It has to work.

00:04:39.850 --> 00:04:42.310
 Oh gosh, maybe it's just
not going to turn out at all.

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:44.860
 Have we lost the spacecraft?

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:48.040
And then the peak
appeared on the graph.

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:50.860
[CHEERING]

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:53.050
It's like, yes, we've got
contact with the spacecraft.

00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:56.431
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:05:07.070 --> 00:05:10.400
NARRATOR: Rosetta began to
send back images of its target.

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:14.180
But after months of seeing
a small dot in the distance,

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:16.115
the comet slowly
came into focus.

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:22.200
 When I saw this
comet crystal clear,

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:25.770
this mountain floating
in space of ice and rock,

00:05:25.770 --> 00:05:27.090
my heart just dropped.

00:05:27.090 --> 00:05:29.190
They were some of the most
dramatic beautiful images

00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:30.450
I have ever seen.

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:34.230
 And then as it
got closer and we

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:37.500
got to see more and more details
on it, yeah, that's when things

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:38.935
started getting really strange.

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:44.880
 Quite simply,
arrival of 67P, we

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:47.580
expected to see something
shaped like a potato,

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:49.500
and we found something
shaped like a rubber duck.

00:05:51.110 --> 00:05:54.500
NARRATOR: 67P is
no ugly duckling,

00:05:54.500 --> 00:05:57.230
but it's strange shape
created a problem for Rosetta.

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:02.630
Orbiting the comet was going
to be far more difficult

00:06:02.630 --> 00:06:03.980
than anyone had imagined.

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:09.490
 A planet has a lot of gravity,
so you can send the spacecraft

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:11.920
out there and then just slow
down a little bit with a rocket

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:14.080
burn and it'll drop into orbit.

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:16.570
In the case of comet
67P, you're dealing

00:06:16.570 --> 00:06:18.630
with a very small little rock.

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:21.220
The spacecraft cannot feel
the gravity of that rock,

00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:22.900
at least not until it's
right up against it.

00:06:24.627 --> 00:06:25.860
 Well, the engineers
had to actually

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:28.210
plot triangular orbits.

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:31.245
It was a very complicated
set of maneuvers.

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:34.860
NARRATOR: Once in orbit,
Rosetta could start work.

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:37.770
Its first task?

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:39.990
Figure out how 67P formed.

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:44.640
 So how did this comet
get this weird shape?

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:46.890
There's two main ideas.

00:06:46.890 --> 00:06:51.790
One is just that it was
eroded somehow in the center,

00:06:51.790 --> 00:06:54.300
and so it started off as
a more spherical thing

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:57.660
and became the shape it is today
through some unknown process.

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:00.330
 The other idea
is that it started

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:01.485
as two separate objects.

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:05.670
NARRATOR: Space rocks
normally hit each other hard.

00:07:08.370 --> 00:07:11.100
They collide with an
average impact speed of more

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:14.070
than 11,000 miles an hour.

00:07:14.070 --> 00:07:16.710
That's five times faster
than a rifle bullet.

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:21.810
Was 67P involved in a pile up?

00:07:21.810 --> 00:07:25.560
A clue came from the distinct
layers on the comet's surface.

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:29.540
 The layers in comet
67P are a little

00:07:29.540 --> 00:07:30.770
like the layers in an onion.

00:07:32.450 --> 00:07:36.320
If you see them aligned that's
a clue that perhaps the object

00:07:36.320 --> 00:07:39.380
formed as a single entity
and only eroded later

00:07:39.380 --> 00:07:41.390
into its present
form, but if you see

00:07:41.390 --> 00:07:43.280
those layers misaligned,
like we actually

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:46.940
do in the comet, that's a big
clue that it started out as two

00:07:46.940 --> 00:07:50.780
separate objects formed
independently sticking together

00:07:50.780 --> 00:07:52.970
to form the comet we see today.

00:07:52.970 --> 00:07:55.100
NARRATOR: The layers
prove that 67P

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:59.870
was originally two separate
objects that fused together.

00:07:59.870 --> 00:08:02.120
 The process of
potentially putting comet

00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:04.730
67P together from
two different pieces

00:08:04.730 --> 00:08:06.110
is important,
because it can teach

00:08:06.110 --> 00:08:09.200
us about what was happening
in the early solar system.

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:13.010
 So as far as we can tell,
these two separate bodies

00:08:13.010 --> 00:08:15.210
must have been formed
in the same area.

00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:17.450
They're very similar
in composition,

00:08:17.450 --> 00:08:19.940
but they are so light
and fluffy that they

00:08:19.940 --> 00:08:23.090
would have destroyed each
other if they had hit fast.

00:08:23.090 --> 00:08:26.420
 They had a low speed collision
and basically stuck together

00:08:26.420 --> 00:08:27.800
like two wet snowballs.

00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:32.330
With one mystery solved,
Rosetta began to investigate

00:08:32.330 --> 00:08:34.084
the chemical makeup of 67B.

00:08:35.780 --> 00:08:38.659
The little comet could answer
one of the biggest questions

00:08:38.659 --> 00:08:41.240
in planetary science,
from where did

00:08:41.240 --> 00:08:43.099
our blue planet get its water?

00:08:43.099 --> 00:08:46.149
[MUSIC PLAYING]

